Let's imagine the fruits of the garden Eden.

Within all fruit is its seed, and within the seed itself the blueprint and material needed to reproduce the very thing which created it.

When a fruit is born and falls from the tree, it decays. Its flesh rots and the seed becomes one with the ground. As the ground takes hold of the seed, it is planted there, it is ready to follow in its fathers footsteps and produce itself anew.

In the Garden of Eden, the story suggests we are offered trees of many fruits which are good for eating. In fact, there is no indication that the Tree of Knowledge had fruit which was not good for eating: Man in the garden is just advised against consuming that particular offering. How much time have we spent considering why this fruit was planted here, as a good experience, but was advised against?

What does it mean to eat a fruit from Eden? What if we considered ourselves to be the ground in which the seed grows, when a fruit has been consumed?

The common Hebrew word for Adam (and mankind) is Adam (אדמ - aleph, dalet, mem). The word for ground is Adamah (אדמה - aleph, dalet, mem, hey). The difference between these two words is the addition of the Hey ה. The meaning of this letter is to "look" or "behold" a thing. Does this make the ground the seen Adam? And if Adam is the unseen ground, then when we consume the fruit, then do we not plant the seed within?